  <h2>Plan the Searches First, Build the Site Second</h2>
  <p>
    Asking a tool to “build me a website” without deciding which searches you are going after is how
    you end up with a good-looking page nobody finds. We work out which terms you can realistically
    win, what order the page runs in, and what the top of the screen has to say. Only then does the
    building start. That is the difference between a brochure and something that brings in work.
  </p>
  <p>
    For the search numbers we use Google’s own Keyword Planner, because it is the only one reading
    real Google searches. The big SEO tools are built for national websites and tend to invent local
    numbers. Your service pages and town pages follow the same rules: a clear heading with the words
    people search, a light mention of the area, and links pointing back to your main page so it gets
    stronger.
  </p>

  <h2>What Most Homeowners Actually See on Your Site</h2>
  <p>
    About four out of five people never scroll far, so the top of the page has to do the selling: who
    you are, why you can be trusted, and an obvious way to call. Everything below answers the
    questions of the people who are still deciding. Your services, the areas you cover, how the job
    runs, what happens if they leave it, common questions, and one more chance to call.
  </p>
